### What does `boxed()` do?

`boxed()` is an intermediate operation on a primitive stream that converts each primitive value into its wrapper object. On an `IntStream` it produces a `Stream<Integer>`; on a `LongStream` a `Stream<Long>`; on a `DoubleStream` a `Stream<Double>`.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class BoxingDemo {
    public static List<Integer> ages(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
            .mapToInt(Employee::getAge) // IntStream of raw ints
            .boxed()                    // Stream<Integer>
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

```text
[35, 29, 42]
```

### Why do you need to box at all?

An `IntStream` has numeric operations but it cannot collect into an object collection, because `Collectors.toList()` and friends operate on `Stream<T>`, not on primitive streams. To use those collectors you must first turn the primitives into objects.

```java
// WRONG: IntStream has no collect(Collectors.toList())
// List<Integer> list = employees.stream().mapToInt(Employee::getAge).collect(Collectors.toList());

// CORRECT: box first, then collect
List<Integer> list = employees.stream()
    .mapToInt(Employee::getAge)
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### How is `boxed()` related to `mapToObj`?

`boxed()` is a convenience method equivalent to `mapToObj(Integer::valueOf)`. Both wrap each `int` into an `Integer`, but `boxed()` is shorter and signals intent.

```java
// These two produce the same Stream<Integer>
IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3).boxed();
IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3).mapToObj(Integer::valueOf);
```

`mapToObj` is more general: it can map an `int` to any object type, not only `Integer`.

```java
// mapToObj mapping ints to a different object type
List<String> labels = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 3)
    .mapToObj(i -> "ACC-" + i)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// [ACC-1, ACC-2, ACC-3]
```

### How does primitive versus boxed compare?

| Aspect | `IntStream` | `Stream<Integer>` (after `boxed()`) |
|--------|-------------|--------------------------------------|
| Element type | raw `int` | `Integer` object |
| Memory | lower, no object headers | higher, one object per element |
| Numeric ops | `sum`, `average`, `min`, `max` | not directly, need `mapToInt` back |
| Object collectors | not available | `collect(toList())`, `toMap`, etc. |
| When to use | numeric aggregation | building object collections |

```java
// Numeric math on the primitive stream, then box only to collect
int[] arr = { 35, 29, 42 };
List<Integer> sortedAges = IntStream.of(arr)
    .filter(age -> age >= 30) // still primitive, fast
    .boxed()                  // box at the boundary
    .sorted()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// [35, 42]
```

### How do you go back from objects to a primitive stream?

The reverse of `boxed()` is `mapToInt`. Starting from a `Stream<Integer>` or a `List<Integer>`, call `mapToInt(Integer::intValue)` to unbox into an `IntStream`.

```java
List<Integer> boxedAges = List.of(35, 29, 42);
int total = boxedAges.stream()
    .mapToInt(Integer::intValue) // unbox back to IntStream
    .sum();
// 106
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The first mistake is boxing too early and doing numeric math on a boxed stream, which pays the boxing cost on every operation.

```java
// WRONG: box first, then every numeric step unboxes and reboxes
int sum = employees.stream()
    .mapToInt(Employee::getAge)
    .boxed()                 // unnecessary here
    .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)
    .sum();
```

```java
// CORRECT: stay primitive for the math, box only if you need objects
int sum = employees.stream()
    .mapToInt(Employee::getAge)
    .sum();
```

The second mistake is trying to call an object collector directly on an `IntStream`, which does not compile.

```java
// WRONG: does not compile, IntStream has no collect(Collector)
// employees.stream().mapToInt(Employee::getAge).collect(Collectors.toList());

// CORRECT: box first
employees.stream().mapToInt(Employee::getAge).boxed().collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### What are the edge cases around the Integer cache?

`Integer.valueOf`, which `boxed()` uses, caches values from -128 to 127, so boxing those reuses shared objects. Outside that range each box creates a new `Integer`. This matters if you ever compare boxed values with `==` instead of `equals`.

```java
Integer a = IntStream.of(100).boxed().findFirst().get();
Integer b = IntStream.of(100).boxed().findFirst().get();
System.out.println(a == b);      // true  (100 is cached)

Integer c = IntStream.of(1000).boxed().findFirst().get();
Integer d = IntStream.of(1000).boxed().findFirst().get();
System.out.println(c == d);      // false (1000 not cached, new objects)
System.out.println(c.equals(d)); // true  (always compare with equals)
```

### When should you use `boxed()` and when should you avoid it?

Use `boxed()` at the boundary where you must produce objects: collecting into a `List<Integer>`, building a map, sorting with a `Comparator`, or passing to an API that expects `Stream<Integer>`. Avoid it in the middle of numeric pipelines where staying primitive is faster.

```java
// Good use: need a List<Integer> result for an API
List<Integer> ages = employees.stream()
    .mapToInt(Employee::getAge)
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Avoid: boxing then immediately summing wastes the primitive advantage
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Boxing cost.** Each box allocates an object (unless cached), adding memory pressure and work for the garbage collector; box once at the end, not per element in a hot path.
- **Three boxed targets.** `IntStream.boxed()` gives `Stream<Integer>`, `LongStream.boxed()` gives `Stream<Long>`, `DoubleStream.boxed()` gives `Stream<Double>`.
- **Equivalence.** `boxed()` equals `mapToObj(Integer::valueOf)`; use `mapToObj` when the target type is not the wrapper.
- **Collectors shortcut.** For a quick list of ints there is also `IntStream.toArray()` returning `int[]` with no boxing at all, which may beat `boxed().collect(toList())` when you only need an array.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What does `boxed()` return on an `IntStream`?" Answer: a `Stream<Integer>`.
- "Why would you call `boxed()`?" Answer: to use object-stream operations like `collect(toList())` or `toMap` that primitive streams lack.
- "What is `boxed()` equivalent to?" Answer: `mapToObj(Integer::valueOf)`.
- "How do you go back to a primitive stream?" Answer: `mapToInt(Integer::intValue)`.
- "Why avoid boxing in numeric pipelines?" Answer: it allocates wrapper objects and loses the performance of primitive streams.
